<section class="py-8 sm:py-16 text-center">
  <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-ink">Red v Blue cyber training</p>
  <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-6xl font-extrabold tracking-tight">
    <span class="text-red-team">Attack</span>. <span class="text-blue-team">Defend</span>. <span class="text-purple-team">Debrief</span>.
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl mx-auto text-lg text-ink-muted">
    Social engineering works because it hides in your normal working day.
    Play the attacker or the defender, and find out which side you prefer.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="how-it-works" aria-labelledby="sides-heading" class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
  <h2 id="sides-heading" class="sr-only">Choose your side</h2>

  <article class="card border-blue-team/50">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-team">Blue team</p>
    <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold">Work a shift</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      You have an inbox, a to-do list and a clock. Pay the invoice, sign the form, reset the
      password. Somewhere in there, Red is waiting. Catch the phish without missing your deadlines.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="card border-red-team/50">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-red-team">Red team</p>
    <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold">Red's Workshop</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      Study a fictional company, then build a phishing email from technique cards on a budget.
      Launch it at a panel of Blue bots and see who you fool.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="card border-purple-team/50 md:col-span-2 md:justify-self-center md:w-[calc(50%-0.75rem)]">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-purple-team">Purple debrief</p>
    <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold">See both sides of every move</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      Every round ends with a replay: the techniques Red used (mapped to MITRE ATT&amp;CK),
      what Blue caught, what slipped through, and the defence that would have stopped it.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>
